## 2. CRITICAL — Position & Capital Ledger Is Disconnected From Reality

This is the single most important finding in this report, and it was **only discoverable by querying the live Alpaca account directly**, which the code itself never does after entry.

### 2.1 Bracket protective orders expire after one trading day

`src/BrokerClient.js:193-241`, `executeBuyOrder()`, submits every bracket order with:
```js
time_in_force: 'day'
```
This applies to the whole bracket, including the `take_profit` (limit) and `stop_loss` (stop) child legs. A `day` order is automatically canceled by the exchange at the close of the session it was submitted in. Live order history pulled from the Alpaca paper account confirms this is exactly what is happening to **every single bracket order the bot has ever submitted**:

| Symbol | Entry date | Stop leg | Take-profit leg |
|---|---|---|---|
| SDOT | 2026-08-24 | `canceled` (same day) | `expired` (same day) |
| ISRL | 2026-09-02 | `canceled` (same day) | `expired` (same day) |
| RDAC | 2026-09-02 | `canceled` (same day) | `expired` (same day) |
| AAPL | 2026-09-09 | `canceled` (same day) | `expired` (same day) |

**A live check of currently-open orders on the account returns zero.** There is not a single active stop-loss or take-profit order protecting any of the bot's current holdings, right now, as of this audit.

Since this strategy holds positions for many days (ISRL/RDAC have been open 12 days; SDOT for 3 weeks), the safety mechanism the whole `RiskEngine.js` / ATR architecture was built around evaporates within hours of every entry. This is very likely the direct cause of SDOT's -51% ($4,662.56 unrealized loss) and WETO's -92% ($15,553.95 unrealized loss, see §2.2) — nothing was watching either position once the entry day ended.

**Fix:** set `time_in_force: 'gtc'` on the bracket order (Alpaca supports GTC brackets), or explicitly set `time_in_force` on the nested `take_profit`/`stop_loss` legs if the parent must stay `day` for the entry itself.

### 2.2 The reconciliation process was deleted and never rebuilt

Git history shows `src/Liquidator.js` existed through 2026-08-20 and was removed in commit `8caf045` ("replace static liquidator with broker-side OCO bracket orders", 2026-08-21), on the reasoning that Alpaca's own bracket orders would handle exits. **What was not carried over: any code that writes the result of an exit back into `trade_analytics`.** Grep across the entire codebase confirms there is no `UPDATE trade_analytics SET status = 'CLOSED'` anywhere in `TradingBot.js`, `BrokerClient.js`, or `Settlement.js`. `Settlement.js` only ever *reads* rows that are already `CLOSED` — nothing ever makes that transition anymore.

The practical result, confirmed by cross-referencing `trade_analytics` against a live `GET /v2/positions` call to Alpaca on 2026-09-14:

| Symbol | Database says | Alpaca (live, paper) says | Reality |
|---|---|---|---|
| **WETO** | `CLOSED`, `net_profit = $0.00` (id 24, backdated to a 2026-09-02 mass "repair") | **Still open.** 549 shares, cost basis $16,827.52, current value $1,273.57 | **-$15,553.95 unrealized loss, completely unbooked, position still live** |
| **SDOT** | `CLOSED`, `net_profit = $0.00` (id 25, same 09-02 repair) | **Still open.** 368 shares, cost basis $9,089.60, current value $4,427.04 | **-$4,662.56 unrealized loss, completely unbooked, position still live** |
| **WEAV** | `CLOSED`, `net_profit = $0.00` (id 5, same 09-02 repair) | **Still open.** 1,128 shares, currently +$14.49 | Small unbooked gain, but the DB has no idea this position exists |
| **RIBBU** | `OPEN` (id 30, opened 2026-09-02, still counted against the 5-slot cap) | **Does not exist in the account.** No position, no order history. | Ghost row artificially consuming one of five position slots for 12 days |
| **AAPL** | **Not present at all** — no row, open or closed | 1 share held, opened via a bracket order 2026-09-09 | A real trade that the database was never told about |
| ISRL | `OPEN` (id 28) | Open, 346 shares, -$132.26 unrealized | Consistent, but unprotected (see §2.1) |
| RDAC | `OPEN` (id 29) | Open, 1,144 shares, -$786.34 unrealized | Consistent, but unprotected (see §2.1) |

Background: the RELEASE-README's 2026-09-03 entry describes a "capacity lock" repair where 5 rows (WEAV, the second WETO row, SDOT, FVNNU, FVN) were manually set to `CLOSED` / `net_profit = $0.00` because they were believed to be dead "ghost positions" blocking the 5-slot cap. **That assumption was wrong for at least WEAV, SDOT, and WETO** — they were not ghosts, they were live positions that got written off the books while still actively running (and, in WETO's case, continued to lose another ~$15k after being marked "closed" at $0).

**Consequences:**
- `capital_pots.active_capital` ($63,237.00, last touched 2026-09-02) has been computed against a fictional position count for 12 days.
- The 5-slot position cap TradingBot.js enforces (`TradingBot.js:31-42`) is being computed from a `trade_analytics` table that both overcounts (RIBBU, a ghost) and undercounts (AAPL is invisible, WEAV/SDOT/WETO are invisible) relative to the real account.
- `Settlement.js` has logged "No trades were closed today" on every single run since 2026-09-03 (see §5) — not because nothing is happening on the account, but because **nothing can ever transition to `CLOSED` anymore**, so there is structurally nothing for Settlement to find.

**This is the top-priority fix.** Before anything else — including the AI/trading-frequency issues below — the system needs a reconciliation job that periodically diffs Alpaca's actual position/order state against `trade_analytics` and corrects it. Until that exists, every number on the dashboard and in `capital_pots` should be treated as unreliable.

