## 3. CRITICAL — Zero New Trades Approved Since the Risk-Debate Launch (2026-09-03)

Statistics pulled directly from `execution.log` (535 recorded trading cycles, 2026-08-19 through 2026-09-14):

| Metric | Count |
|---|---|
| Trading cycles run | 535 |
| Trades successfully executed | **20 — all between 2026-08-19 and 2026-08-25** |
| Trades executed since the Agent Debate went live (2026-09-03 onward) | **0** |
| Agent B (`Risk Auditor`) explicit `APPROVED` verdicts (all time) | **0** |
| Agent B explicit `REJECTED` verdicts | 38 |
| Cycles where Agent A itself hard-failed (`Agent A CRITICAL`) | 66 |

All 20 real trades in the database predate the two-agent debate entirely (`AIEngine.js`'s `auditTradeSetup` / Agent B was introduced in commit `0bb514b`, 2026-09-03). Since then, **not one proposal has cleared Agent B**, out of at least 38 proposals that reached it.

### 3.1 Root cause: volume data is never actually collected

Grepping the Risk Auditor's own rejection notes shows the dominant, recurring reason:

> "Volume is undefined, making it difficult to assess slippage risk..."
> "Audit revealed critical data gaps with undefined volume..."
> "Thin volume not provided for MKDW..."
> "Volume data is undefined, making it impossible to assess slippage risk..."

This phrase (or a close variant) appears in the majority of the 38 rejections. Looking at `src/BrokerClient.js:108-116`, the market-data object sent to the AI is built as:
```js
validSetups.push({
    symbol: mover.symbol,
    price: mover.price,
    dailyChange: mover.percent_change.toFixed(2),
    volume: mover.volume,        // <-- this field
    sma_20: sma_20,
    rsi_14: this.calculateRSI(closePrices, 14),
    rawBars: bars
});
```
`mover` comes straight from Alpaca's `/v1beta1/screener/stocks/movers` response (`BrokerClient.js:49-61`), which does not appear to include a `volume` field for each mover in its schema — hence `mover.volume` resolves to `undefined` for effectively every candidate, every cycle. That literal `undefined` gets string-interpolated straight into the Agent A/Agent B prompts (`AIEngine.js:132`, `:182`: `Volume: ${marketData.volume}`), so the model is *correctly* told there is no volume data and is *correctly* skeptical of every setup as a result. This is not the AI "being too conservative" — it's the AI faithfully doing its job on top of a data pipeline that was never actually wired up.

The bars endpoint the code already calls (`BrokerClient.js:80-92`, used for SMA/RSI) returns Alpaca's standard OHLCV bar shape (`o,h,l,c,v,t,n,vw`), i.e. **the volume data is one line away** — `bars[bars.length - 1].v` (or an average over the recent window) — it is simply never read out of a response the code is already fetching.

**Fix:** populate `volume` from the `bars` array instead of the nonexistent `mover.volume`. This alone is very likely to unblock the majority of currently-stuck proposals.

### 3.2 Position-slot math is also corrupted by §2.2

Even once Agent B starts approving trades again, `TradingBot.js:31-42` computes `neededSlots = 5 - openPositions` from the same desynced `trade_analytics` table documented in §2.2 — currently reporting 3/5 held, when Alpaca actually shows 6 real positions. Fixing §3.1 without first fixing §2.2 will make the bot open trades against a capacity number that doesn't reflect the real account.

